\section*{Regularity Properties of Nuclear \$C\textasciicircum{}*\$-Algebras}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): A separable nuclear C*-algebra that is simple and non-elementary is claimed to absorb Jiang--Su under extended-functional strict comparison, including nonunital and unbounded-trace cases; semigroup divisibility also yields a nonsimple criterion. Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Finite nuclear dimension is claimed to characterize Jiang--Su stability for separable nuclear algebras lacking elementary ideal subquotients; moreover, Jiang--Su stabilization always lowers nuclear dimension to at most one. Manuscript 3 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The paper claims uniform Gamma from real rank zero in the tracial-completion ultrapower for simple unital nuclear stably finite algebras. Comparison gives absorption, also under specified bounded-trace hypotheses in the stably projectionless setting.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Trace transfer into small hereditary supports followed by spectral rounding establishes full Cuntz-semigroup divisibility. Pure-state compression produces norm-visible matrix cones, while localized nuclear approximations produce trace-visible ones. Comparison packs their defects into a central corner, constructing dimension-drop maps whose lifted intertwining relations give Jiang--Su absorption.

Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Localized order-zero approximations control ranks even when traces are infinite on some ideals. A supporting map and comparison yield two-color approximations that survive stabilization and hereditary passage. Conversely, Gaussian sign tensors and pure-state excision construct two full orthogonal central elements from the absence of characters, supplying the absorption criterion.

Manuscript 3 (family 291, 2026-09-23): Nuclear averaging and scalar block compressions preserve coefficient traces while packing small projections with controlled commutators. A separate comparison construction rounds ranks, forms clipped sums of centered symmetries, and extracts Gaussian trace partitions. In the projectionless case, a combined overlap-and-model cost controls small splittings before reindexing amplifies them to exact central partitions.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The strict-comparison/Jiang–Su/nuclear-dimension claims do not settle the additional finite-decomposition-rank equivalence for all stably finite algebras requested by this record.

Manuscript 2 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The strict-comparison/Jiang–Su/nuclear-dimension claims do not settle the additional finite-decomposition-rank equivalence for all stably finite algebras requested by this record.

Manuscript 3 (family 291, 2026-09-23): The strict-comparison/Jiang–Su/nuclear-dimension claims do not settle the additional finite-decomposition-rank equivalence for all stably finite algebras requested by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 291, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection gives uniform Gamma from real rank zero of the designated tracial ultrapower. The documented comparison scope also treats Jiang--Su absorption and Gamma under strict comparison, with additional trace and target-rank conditions in the projectionless case. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause. Manuscript 2 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 3. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause. Comparator files: UniformGamma.lean. Declarations: OAI.ComparatorModel.CurrentMain.main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

